From 0aa6991b5e4a19ca4774b4bf09ce9adf05911ded Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zed-patches <zed-patches@localhost>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 15:49:29 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] browser_extension: show and drive the same agent thread from
 a browser sidebar

Reading what the agent did while looking at the page it was building meant
switching windows, and pointing it at "this button on this page" meant copying
DOM by hand. zeo-browser (a local Chrome/Firefox extension, built from
zed/zeo-browser) puts the Zeo thread in the browser's side panel: it follows the
thread of the most recently active Zeo window (or one the operator pins), streams
its entries, and sends messages carrying picked elements, screenshots and, on
Chrome, recorded console/network captures. It is the same thread, never a second
session: whatever the sidebar sends lands in Zeo's own queue and transcript.

The path is one ingress. The browser starts the new zeo-browser-host binary
(crates/zeo_browser_host, installed to /usr/libexec by the ebuild, with Chrome,
Chromium and Firefox native-messaging manifests) over stdio; the host relays each
frame as one line to the devtools bridge socket of 0052
($XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/zeo/devtools.sock). No port is opened anywhere.

Wire contract v2 (the shared fixtures in zeo-browser/test/contract are the
oracle; a Rust agreement test reads them through ZEO_BROWSER_CONTRACT_DIR):
- the bridge reads a connection's first line: version 1 keeps 0052's one-shot
  request/response unchanged; a v2 hello opens a long-lived session;
- client kinds: hello, list_threads, subscribe {follow | thread_id},
  send {text, attachments}, cancel, authorize, open_in_zeo;
- server kinds: approval_pending, welcome, threads, snapshot (last 100 entries,
  one line <= 1 000 000 bytes, `earlier` counts the rest), entry_added,
  entry_updated (coalesced to <= 1 per 100 ms per entry), entries_removed,
  status, title, permission_requested/resolved, target_changed, thread_closed,
  sent {queued, images}, ok, error {error, message, fatal};
- status.state is idle, working, unconfirmed, awaiting_permission or error.
  `unconfirmed` (0.6.1_p20261021) is a resumed ACP v2 session whose activity
  the agent has not reported yet, upstream's ThreadStatus::Unconfirmed; it
  read `idle` before. zeo-browser's contract.ts must list it: an extension
  whose schema does not drops every threads, snapshot and status message
  that carries it;
- a browser send is a manual send: it waits in Zeo's queue only while a turn
  is generating or the window is still resolving a send, and goes out at once
  on an Unconfirmed thread, as the composer's send button does;
- entry indices are the thread's own, never snapshot-relative;
- attachments are validated in Zeo against the contract limits (counts, sizes,
  canonical padded base64, image magic and dimensions, http(s)/file page URLs)
  before agent_ui sees them; one broken limit refuses the whole message.

Security model:
- approval: the first hello of an unknown (browser, extension id) shows "A
  browser extension (<browser>, <id>) wants to read and drive your agent
  threads." with Allow and Deny in the most recently active window; Allow is
  persisted (key zeo_browser_approved_clients), Deny holds for the run, the
  prompt times out after 120 s, closing it ends the wait. Every message before
  approval is answered not_approved. The command "devtools bridge: revoke
  browser access" forgets every identity and closes every session; a revoke
  generation orders it against an Allow still being written;
- the boundary is the same uid: the host overwrites the identity in every
  hello from its argv, which only the browser controls, but any process of the
  operator's user can reach the socket, as with 0052;
- page content is untrusted: element, console and network chips render inside
  <untrusted-page-content source="browser page" url="..."> with 0052's note;
  page text cannot close or open the block (tag name neutralised in any case,
  width or dash form, Unicode line breaks and control characters folded);
  labels built from page data are sanitised; a chip opens nothing on click;
- a permission choice made in the browser resolves through the window's own
  resolver, so a pattern choice ("Always for `cargo` commands") keeps its
  pattern and never becomes a tool-wide default;
- logs are key=value and never carry URLs, message text or page content.

Compatibility: a thread holding a browser-context chip reloads over ACP on
upstream Zed and older Zeo with the chip dropped and its text kept. A native-
agent thread does not: MentionUri has no serde fallback, so a Zeo downgraded
below this patch fails to load that thread (0052's dom-element chip has the same
property). Upstream Zed uses its own data directory and is not affected.

Type placement: agent_ui cannot depend on devtools_bridge, so the v2 wire types
live in agent_ui::browser_wire, and 0052's DomSummary/Rect moved there with them
(devtools_bridge re-exports both; behaviour unchanged).

USE browser-extension (default on) needs devtools-bridge. Applies on top of
0052 with or without 0057; 0050/0051 apply on top of it unchanged.

Story 045 (zed/.epic/stories/045-browser-extension), branch dev-045.

Story 064 (2026-11-02): a thread snapshot carries `subagents: [{title,
status}]`, one entry per registered child in registration order, omitted when
the thread has none; same-titled children stay two entries. zeo-browser 0.1.x
parses the server schema strictly and drops a snapshot carrying the field;
0.2.0 accepts it. 7 tests (browser_wire/subagents_tests.rs,
browser_channel_tests/browser_wire_subagents.rs).
